speechtext:
Perhaps, if they had been in Montreal, they would have run a little more rife than they did in Toronto. The point that I had in mind was that some sort of drastic tax should be imposed on these people if they do not intend to become Canadian citizens. If they intend to become Canadian citizens, to secure a stake in this country, to stay here, to work on our farms
or in our large centres, if they intend to raise families here and will assimilate, let them come; the more of those that come, the better I shall be pleased. But if they are going to be only transients, I would say: "Tax them to the hilt." I am glad the Minister of Finance (Mr. Fielding) is in his seat at the present time. He was in a bad way this afternoon wondering whether or not this Budget which he brought down would provide sufficient money wherewith to run this country. Let him tax these people. From my experience with them-and I have had considerable experience with them- they would be submissive to this tax; they would still come to our country and work in our factories and pay this tax and, perhaps, be glad to do so. That might be a suggestion, although only a small one, for the Minister of Finance in regard to taxation. To-day I received a resolution passed by the Union of Canadian Municipalities at their 1921 convention, and with your permission, Sir, I should like to read it because it is thoroughly in accord with my ideas in this matter. The resolution reads:
Whereas representations have been made to the Union of Canadian Municipalities that an apparently organized campaign of propaganda is now under way with the obvious purpose of resisting public objection to foreign immigration and alleging that the restrictions upon immigration by the United States of America provide a golden opportunity for Canada to increase its population ;
And whereas the reasons which impelled the United States to impose almost prohibitive restrictions upon intending immigrants to that country apply with equal if not added force to conditions in Canada ;
And whereas it is not seemly that Canada should receive as future citizens persons of foreign birth, who are considered undesirable by a neighboring country, especially in view of the unfavourable experience of our larger cities with the disproportionate foreign population which they already have;
Be it resolved that the Union of Canadian Municipalities regards with serious apprehension the efforts of transportation interests to encourage the immigration into Canada of persons of alien birth more particularly in view of the unemployment problems now confronting this country, and directs that a petition be addressed to the Federal Government asking that the Immigration Act be amended to prohibit further immigration of aliens for urban occupations and to ensure that such aliens as may be admitted to Canada shall be bona fide settlers on the land and capable of successfully establishing themselves in that capacity, and that the official, medical and other examination of intending immigrants be made at the port of embarkation, instead of the Canadian port of entry as at present.
I might add that I have had a resolution from the Ontario Convention of the Great War Veterans' Association along the same line, more particularly with regard to the
Supply-Immigration

taxation of transient aliens. In conclusion, Mr. Chairman, I would ask the minister who has charge of the Immigration Department (Mr. Stewart) whether he cannot give us a little clearer idea as to the policy of the Government with reference to the suggested discouragement of transient alien immigration.
